;;; `(absent ())` loads, and holds — regression
;;; `absent-empty` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule whose `:match` writes `(absent ())` beside its
;;; premise `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `()` is a whole `:match` or a whole `:assert` and nothing else, so
;;; `(absent ())` is refused: every command that reads the program exits 1
;;; (`ein test`: 2). Read as an empty `:match`, `()` is true (Q-M1e2.36), so
;;; its absence would never hold.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded and `(absent ())` held, so `rr` fired for every `p`:
;;; root derived `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, H was empty and R {∅}, exit 0
;;; (`ein saturate FILE --dump`, `ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at
;;; `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike). The engine
;;; now refuses it at the parse, on every command.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p T) (relation hit T)
(p a) (p b)
(rule rr () :match (and (p ?x) (absent ())) :assert (hit ?x))
(query :goal (hit ?x))
